{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0183tV9RzdoXahq7bLxvXaga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experimental data for the \"worst\" tier scenario for each planned experiment in their experiment plan. Let me carefully analyze the worst tier definition and generate realistic synthetic data for each experiment.\n\nKey worst tier characteristics:\n- Specialized LoRA barely outperforms or matches universal LoRA\n- Core routing hypothesis doesn't produce meaningful downstream improvement\n- libero_plus_overall_meta_brain: [64, 69]\n- gain_over_frozen: [10, 15]\n- universal_lora_overall: [62, 67]\n\n\n- gain_over_universal_lora: [0, 4]\n- per_type_specialization_delta_max: [1, 6]\n- oracle_par_gap: [1, 5]\n- routing_diagonality: [0.45, 0.7]\n- gain_per_million_params: [1.7, 2.5]\n- cm_ablation_delta: [-1, 1]\n\nNow I'm looking at the metric ranges for the worst-case scenario and preparing to generate synthetic data across all twelve planned experiments, where the narrative shifts toward probing VLA representations as the main contribution while routing becomes an informative negative result.\n\nI'm working through the baseline numbers to ensure consistency: the frozen pi0 sits at 53.6%, universal LoRA pushes to around 64.8% for an 11.2% gain, and Meta-Brain reaches 66.4% with only a 1.6% marginal improvement—keeping the story focused on the modest gains from routing while the real value lies elsewhere.\n\nNow I'm filling in the supporting metrics: oracle routing caps out around 68.6%, per-type specialization differences stay modest at 4.1%, routing weights cluster around 0.58 diagonality, and the efficiency metric checks out at 2.13 gain per million parameters. I'm also setting the context module ablation at 0.4% to show it's a minor contributor.\n\nWith those anchors in place, I'm breaking down how the perturbation types in LIBERO-Plus distribute across visual, spatial, and physical categories to build out the per-type performance deltas. I need to focus on the planned experiments rather than getting lost in the per-type breakdown. Plan_001 is already running with mostly complete results, though a few metrics like few_shot_k1 and sensor_noise are still pending, so I should include those for completeness. The key insight is that Phase 0 is actually performing well even in the worst tier—the real challenge emerges in Phase 1. For plan_002, I'm expecting the specialized LoRA experts to show only marginal improvements over the universal approach on their own types, with cross-type performance revealing how interchangeable these experts really are. Plan_003 with the Meta-Brain assembly is where things get interesting, but I need to think through what the overall results should demonstrate.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining experiments: plan_004 shows that even oracle routing provides minimal gains over PAR, suggesting routing itself isn't the bottleneck. The ablation ladder in plan_005 is the core evidence I need. Plan_006 hints that FTM might actually outperform LoRA on certain visual perturbations. Plan_007 reveals Meta-Brain's efficiency gains are modest compared to the universal baseline. Plans_008 through plan_010 all point to the same pattern—component ablations show minimal impact, routing architecture doesn't matter much, and the 6-class versus 3-class distinction is negligible. Plan_011 is encouraging because Phase 0 replicates well across backbones, which supports the pivot narrative. Plan_012 shows layer placement has minimal effect. I'm about to structure all of this into detailed YAML output.\n\nNow I need to establish the frozen baseline numbers per perturbation type to keep everything internally consistent. I'm setting the overall frozen performance at 53.6% as mentioned in the paper framing, then deriving per-type breakdowns that average to that number while maintaining reasonable variation across camera, light, background, sensor noise, layout, and robot perturbations.\n\nFor the Universal LoRA approach, I'm targeting an overall performance of around 64.8%, with each perturbation type showing improvement over the frozen baseline while staying proportionally consistent. Then I'll move on to defining the Specialized LoRA experts that perform on their own perturbation types.\n\nNow I'm looking at how each specialized expert performs when applied to its intended type—LoRA-V handles the visual perturbations with gains of 1-3 points, LoRA-S focuses on layout with a 4-point improvement, and LoRA-P handles the robot with a 1.2-point boost. The maximum specialization delta is 4.0, which fits within the acceptable range. I'm also checking cross-type performance to see how much each expert degrades when applied to the wrong perturbation type.\n\nLooking at the Meta-Brain routing results, it achieves an overall score of 66.4 across all perturbation types, which is 1.6 points above the universal baseline. The Oracle routing reaches 68.6, leaving a gap of 2.2 points—this tells me there's still room for improvement in the routing strategy. The efficiency metric shows Meta-Brain gains 2.13 points per million parameters, which is solid. The routing diagonality of 0.58 indicates good specialization without being too extreme, and adding the context module provides only a marginal 0.4-point improvement, so it's not critical. Now I'm moving on to evaluate the FTM approach on visual perturbations.\n\nFTM performs comparably to Meta-Brain on visual types, averaging 65.0 versus Meta-Brain's 65.5, though it shows mixed results across individual perturbations—it outperforms on camera and light but underperforms on background and sensor noise. Since FTM isn't designed for non-visual perturbations, I need to compile the full YAML output with all the planned experiment results.\n\nLooking at the experiment statuses, plan_001 is currently running while plans 002 through 012 are all marked as planned, so I should focus on providing detailed data for those planned experiments. I also need to include the pending metrics from plan_001 like the few_shot_k1 layer6 result and sensor noise recheck, though the user specifically asked for planned experiments rather than the running one.\n\nNow I'm organizing the output structure around the four worst-tier hypotheses: that universal LoRA implicitly learns perturbation-conditional representations, that rank-16 LoRA is insufficient for type-specific behaviors, that PAR routing weights remain near-uniform, and that there's a fundamental performance ceiling. I'll draft comprehensive YAML with detailed tables, figures, and analysis for each of the planned experiments.\n\nI need to be careful about the per-type breakdown for LIBERO-Plus — aggregating across task suites by perturbation type rather than getting granular. For the statistical reporting, I'm thinking moderate standard deviations for the worst tier with overlapping confidence intervals between specialized and universal approaches, which explains why the gains aren't statistically significant. Something like Universal LoRA at 64.8 ± 2.1 and Meta-Brain at 66.4 ± 1.8 would show that overlap clearly.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the full YAML with all required fields for each experiment.\n\nFor plan_002, I need to set up a proper cross-evaluation matrix showing per-expert success rates both on their own type and across different types. I should also verify that Phase 0 routing in the worst tier is still performing excellently — the key results show F1=1.0 at layer 6 with K=1, so the routing itself is solid even though it doesn't translate downstream. Now I'm compiling the 5-seed results for the ablation ladder across all methods. Looking at these results, the Meta-Brain gain over universal LoRA isn't statistically significant — it's basically noise. For the 6-class routing experiment, adding more experts doesn't help since each one has less capacity to work with. The cross-backbone tests show routing provides minimal benefit even on the smaller model, and layer placement doesn't matter much either, suggesting the LoRA is doing general correction rather than something specific to certain layer types. Now I'm structuring this into the final output format with plan details and expected results.\n\nGenerating markdown table... I'm recalculating the visual perturbation averages to verify the numbers work out — FTM comes in at 66.2%, which is actually slightly ahead of Meta-Brain's 65.5%, so Meta-Brain's real advantage is its coverage across all perturbation types rather than superiority on any single category. Now I'm ready to write out the final YAML with these corrected metrics.\n\nFor the ablation ladder table, I'm laying out the performance across all perturbation types with the frozen baseline, universal LoRA, Meta-Brain, and oracle methods — though I'm noticing something odd with the oracle's robot performance that needs checking.\n\nLet me recalculate what oracle routing actually means: it should select the best specialized expert for each perturbation type based on ground truth, so the oracle performance per type equals the specialized expert's performance on that type. Working through the numbers — camera at 60.0, light at 69.0, background at 64.0, sensor noise at 71.0, layout at 66.0, and robot at 72.0 — the overall oracle score comes out to 67.0 when I average across all six types.\n\nNow I'm adjusting these numbers to ensure the oracle-PAR gap falls within the expected [1, 5] range. Since Meta-Brain is at 66.4, I need oracle to be between 67.4 and 71.4. Tweaking the specialized expert scores slightly upward — camera to 61.0, light to 70.0, background to 65.0, sensor noise to 72.0, layout stays at 66.0, and robot to 73.0 — gives me an oracle average of 67.83, which creates a gap of 1.4 from Meta-Brain. That's on the low end of the range, which makes sense for the worst-case tier where even perfect routing barely improves performance.\n\nI should also adjust Meta-Brain's per-type scores to be consistent with this framework, since it uses soft routing and should fall somewhere between the universal baseline and the oracle ceiling.\n\nLooking at the per-type differences between universal and oracle, I'm seeing gaps of 4, 2, 4, 2, 4, and 2.2 across the six types. With a routing diagonality of 0.58, Meta-Brain would capture roughly 40-60% of that oracle-universal gap. So I'm scaling each type by about 50% of its potential improvement — camera goes from 57 to 59, light from 68 to 69, background from 61 to 63, sensor noise from 70 to 71, layout from 62 to 64, and robot stays at 70.8.\n\nAveraging those per-type scores gives me an overall Meta-Brain performance of 66.3, which is close to my earlier estimate of 66.4. The oracle overall is 67.8, so Meta-Brain gains 12.7 points over the frozen baseline and 1.5 points over universal routing, with a 1.5-point gap remaining to oracle. The efficiency metric checks out at 2.12 gain per million parameters.\n\nNow I'm setting up the cross-type evaluation matrix to see how each expert performs when different perturbation types are applied, starting with the camera, lighting, background, noise, layout, and robot categories.\n\nLooking at the results, the universal LoRA holds its own remarkably well—it's often competitive with or even outperforming the specialized experts on mismatched perturbation types. The visual expert struggles on layout tasks (58.0 vs universal's 62.0), and the spatial and pose experts similarly underperform on visual perturbations, but these gaps are surprisingly narrow. The real insight is that while experts do show some specialization, the gains are modest and come with cross-type penalties that require precise routing to exploit—and even with perfect routing, the oracle only reaches 67.8 compared to universal's 64.8, a relatively small margin.\n\nNow I'm verifying the oracle gain calculation and moving into the YAML output. Let me also review the seed-level statistics for the frozen, universal, and Meta-Brain configurations to ensure the variance estimates are accurate.\n\nThe Meta-Brain improvement over universal in the worst tier is marginal at 1.5 points with high uncertainty, and the paired t-test shows this isn't statistically significant. This is the key finding—routing doesn't meaningfully help in this tier.\n\nNow I'm shifting to plan_009 to compare different router architectures, and I'm seeing that across MLP, linear, and attention-based routing, the downstream performance in the worst tier stays relatively flat around 66%, suggesting the routing mechanism itself isn't the bottleneck.\n\nFor plan_010, I tested 3-class versus 6-class expert configurations with the same total parameter budget, and the finer 6-class split actually underperforms because halving the rank per expert outweighs any benefit from more granular routing. Plan_011 shows the findings hold up on SmolVLA as well—the Phase 0 routing quality is slightly lower than on pi0 but still strong, and the Phase 1 gain follows the same pattern, which validates the approach across different backbones.\n\nPlan_012 tests different layer placements for the mixture of experts, and they all converge around 65-66% regardless of whether I put them early, mid, late, or everywhere—so the specific layer positioning doesn't seem to matter, which contradicts my initial hypothesis about type-specific placement.\n\nNow I'm compiling all the results to write out the comprehensive YAML. Looking at plan_006 for FTM reproduction, the visual perturbation results show FTM at 66.2%, LoRA-V essentially matching at 66.0%, Meta-Brain at 65.5%, and the universal baseline at 64.0%—FTM achieves this with only 4K parameters for the visual component but lacks spatial and physical reasoning capabilities.\n\nThe parameter efficiency comparison is actually problematic for my narrative: FTM gains 14.45% over frozen baseline using just 4K params on visual, while Meta-Brain gains 12.7% over frozen on all perturbation types but requires 6M params, meaning FTM is more efficient per parameter despite being narrower in scope.\n\nBefore I finalize the YAML, I need to clarify what per_type_specialization_delta_max actually represents—it should be the maximum improvement when a specialized expert handles its own perturbation type versus the universal baseline, so I'm checking the numbers: camera shows 4.0 point improvement, light shows 2.0, background shows 4.0.\n\nComposing the YAML output now...\n\nI need to verify the perturbation type count for LIBERO-Plus—I'm seeing 6 distinct categories (camera, light, background, sensor noise, layout, robot) rather than 7, so I'll stick with that breakdown for the per-perturbation metrics. So I'm settling on 6 perturbation types aligned with the experiment taxonomy, treating the \"7 categories\" mention as likely including a clean baseline. Now I need to write out the full results with detailed metrics and tables for each planned experiment, keeping it thorough without unnecessary padding. I'm also thinking through the CM ablation logic—the routing-weight modulation from exemplars should have minimal effect in worst-case scenarios where routing itself is already struggling.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Generating comparison table...\n\nNow I'm calculating the efficiency metrics—Meta-Brain gets 0.25% gain per million parameters while VLA-GSE achieves 0.105% per million, which actually suggests Meta-Brain is more parameter-efficient despite the smaller absolute gains.\n\nLooking at gain-over-frozen instead paints a different picture: Universal LoRA dominates at 5.60% per million parameters, while Meta-Brain sits at 2.12% and VLA-GSE at roughly 0.247%, though that last comparison isn't quite fair since the backbones differ. The scatter plot would show that routing adds minimal marginal benefit for triple the parameter cost.\n\nI've worked through the numbers enough—time to write out the YAML. I should also make sure to include standard deviations for the per-seed data in the plan_005 tables.\n\nNow I'm looking at the error distributions across conditions. The frozen baseline sits at 53.6 ± 0.9, Universal LoRA reaches 64.8 ± 1.1, Meta-Brain gets to 66.3 ± 1.1, and Oracle tops out at 67.8 ± 1.0. The difference between Meta-Brain and Universal is only 1.5 ± 1.5 and doesn't reach significance, while Oracle's gain over Universal is 3.0 ± 0.8 and statistically significant but still modest. This reinforces the worst-tier story: even with perfect routing, we're only looking at a 3% improvement, and learned routing gives us essentially nothing.\n\nLet me finalize the YAML now and verify all the constraints are satisfied.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11382,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11382},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7b30f17-3885-4360-950b-9073de7caf33","uuid":"ab6bf41f-932d-48cc-b807-c6348e33e02a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0183tV9RzdoXahq7bLxvXaga","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Paper pivots from 'routing improves correction' to 'what VLA representations reveal about perturbation processing'.\n  Lead contribution becomes Phase 0 mechanistic discovery (F1=1.0 at K=1 single layer). Contribution 2 becomes\n  a systematic negative-but-informative result: explicit perturbation-diagnostic routing yields only marginal,\n  statistically non-significant gains over implicit mixed-perturbation LoRA training, providing evidence that\n  rank-16 LoRA mixed training already captures perturbation-conditional structure. Universal LoRA is the\n  surprising finding — it implicitly learns type-aware correction without any routing signal. Title shifts to\n  'Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing'.\n  Key framing: the routing signal is real (Phase 0 proves it), but the correction bottleneck is expert capacity,\n  not routing quality. This reframes the contribution as a design-dimension analysis that maps out the landscape\n  for future work with higher-rank experts, multi-backbone settings, or compositional perturbation scenarios.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  - name: \"LoRA expert training — 3 specialized + 1 universal\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      Train 4 LoRA adapters (rank 16) on frozen pi0 PaliGemma: LoRA-V (Visual), LoRA-S (Spatial),\n      LoRA-P (Physical), Universal (all types mixed). Evaluate each on all perturbation types.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_on_camera: 61.0\n      lora_v_on_light: 70.0\n      lora_v_on_background: 65.0\n      lora_v_on_sensor_noise: 72.0\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 67.0\n      lora_s_on_layout: 66.0\n      lora_p_on_robot: 73.0\n      universal_on_camera: 57.0\n      universal_on_light: 68.0\n      universal_on_background: 61.0\n      universal_on_sensor_noise: 70.0\n      universal_on_layout: 62.0\n      universal_on_robot: 70.8\n      universal_overall: 64.8\n      max_specialization_delta: 4.0\n      lora_v_on_layout_cross: 58.0\n      lora_v_on_robot_cross: 65.0\n      lora_s_on_visual_avg_cross: 59.5\n      lora_p_on_visual_avg_cross: 60.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Specialized experts show only marginal gains over universal on their own types (max +4.0% on\n      background/layout, +2.0–2.2% on light/noise/robot). Root cause: rank-16 LoRA has limited correction\n      capacity (~2M params per expert), and at this rank the parameter space is too constrained to learn\n      meaningfully different type-specific behaviors. All experts converge to similar low-rank corrections\n      that primarily recover the frozen backbone's general robustness gap. The universal LoRA, trained on\n      mixed perturbation data, implicitly learns a perturbation-conditional representation — mixed training\n      acts as a form of implicit multi-task learning that covers the type-specific corrections nearly as well\n      as explicit specialization. Cross-type degradation exists (LoRA-V on layout drops to 58.0 vs universal 62.0)\n      but is moderate, further indicating that experts are not highly differentiated.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-evaluation matrix: Expert × Perturbation Type (success rate %)\"\n        content: |\n          | Expert      | Camera | Light | BG   | Noise | Layout | Robot | Avg   |\n          |-------------|--------|-------|------|-------|--------|-------|-------|\n          | LoRA-V      | **61.0** | **70.0** | **65.0** | **72.0** | 58.0 | 65.0 | 65.2 |\n          | LoRA-S      | 53.0   | 63.0  | 57.0 | 65.0  | **66.0** | 67.0 | 61.8 |\n          | LoRA-P      | 54.0   | 64.0  | 58.0 | 66.0  | 60.0   | **73.0** | 62.5 |\n          | Universal   | 57.0   | 68.0  | 61.0 | 70.0  | 62.0   | 70.8  | 64.8 |\n          | Frozen pi0  | 44.0   | 56.0  | 49.0 | 58.0  | 52.0   | 62.6  | 53.6 |\n          Bold = expert matched to own perturbation type.\n      - title: \"Specialization delta: Specialized − Universal per type\"\n        content: |\n          | Type        | Specialized | Universal | Delta |\n          |-------------|-------------|-----------|-------|\n          | Camera      | 61.0        | 57.0      | +4.0  |\n          | Light       | 70.0        | 68.0      | +2.0  |\n          | Background  | 65.0        | 61.0      | +4.0  |\n          | Noise       | 72.0        | 70.0      | +2.0  |\n          | Layout      | 66.0        | 62.0      | +4.0  |\n          | Robot       | 73.0        | 70.8      | +2.2  |\n          | Max delta   |             |           | 4.0   |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Grouped bar chart: per-perturbation success rate for each expert + universal. Shows experts are only slightly above universal on matched types and below on mismatched types.\"\n      - \"Heatmap of (Expert × Type) deltas relative to universal, highlighting the narrow specialization corridor.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The cross-evaluation matrix reveals that rank-16 LoRA experts converge to similar correction strategies.\n      Universal LoRA averages 64.8% overall, while matched experts average 67.8% on their own types — a\n      difference of only 3.0%. Critically, mismatched experts often underperform universal (LoRA-V on layout:\n      58.0 vs 62.0; LoRA-S on visual avg: 59.5 vs 64.0), meaning routing must be accurate just to avoid\n      degradation. This creates a narrow \"routing benefit window\" where the expert-universal delta (~2–4%) must\n      exceed the routing-error cost. At rank-16, the expert capacity bottleneck limits the maximum extractable\n      benefit from routing, regardless of routing quality.\n\n  - name: \"Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      Assemble PAR router (layer 6 MLP) with 3 LoRA experts under soft routing. End-to-end routing\n      fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_overall: 66.3\n      gain_over_frozen: 12.7\n      gain_over_universal: 1.5\n      per_type_camera: 59.0\n      per_type_light: 69.0\n      per_type_background: 63.0\n      per_type_noise: 71.0\n      per_type_layout: 64.0\n      per_type_robot: 72.0\n      routing_diagonality: 0.58\n      routing_weight_visual_on_visual: 0.52\n      routing_weight_spatial_on_spatial: 0.61\n      routing_weight_physical_on_physical: 0.63\n      routing_entropy_mean: 0.89\n    result_rationale: >\n      Soft routing weights are near-uniform (diagonality 0.58, mean entropy 0.89 vs max 1.10 for 3-class\n      uniform). The PAR router correctly classifies perturbation type (Phase 0 F1=0.993) but the learned\n      routing-to-correction mapping during fine-tuning converges toward distributing weight across all experts\n      rather than concentrating on the matched one. This happens because the expert outputs are too similar\n      (rank-16 convergence from plan_002) — the optimization landscape has no strong gradient toward diagonal\n      routing since all experts provide nearly equal correction. The resulting Meta-Brain captures most of the\n      universal LoRA gain (12.7 vs 11.2 over frozen) but adds only 1.5% from routing, which is within the\n      noise floor of 5-seed evaluation.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"PAR routing weight matrix (rows = true type, columns = expert weight)\"\n        content: |\n          | True Type | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\n          |-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n          | Visual    | **0.52**  | 0.24      | 0.24      | 0.94    |\n          | Spatial   | 0.21      | **0.61**  | 0.18      | 0.83    |\n          | Physical  | 0.19      | 0.18      | **0.63**  | 0.80    |\n          | Diag avg  |           |           |           | 0.58    |\n          Perfect diagonality = 1.0, uniform = 0.33. Observed 0.58 = weak preference.\n      - title: \"Meta-Brain per-perturbation results\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation | Frozen | Universal | Meta-Brain | Δ(MB−Uni) |\n          |--------------|--------|-----------|------------|-----------|\n          | Camera       | 44.0   | 57.0      | 59.0       | +2.0      |\n          | Light        | 56.0   | 68.0      | 69.0       | +1.0      |\n          | Background   | 49.0   | 61.0      | 63.0       | +2.0      |\n          | Noise        | 58.0   | 70.0      | 71.0       | +1.0      |\n          | Layout       | 52.0   | 62.0      | 64.0       | +2.0      |\n          | Robot        | 62.6   | 70.8      | 72.0       | +1.2      |\n          | **Overall**  | **53.6** | **64.8** | **66.3**   | **+1.5**  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Routing weight heatmap (3×3): near-uniform rather than diagonal, with max diagonal entry only 0.63.\"\n      - \"Per-perturbation stacked bar: Frozen → Universal gain (large) vs Universal → Meta-Brain gain (thin sliver) visually demonstrates marginal routing benefit.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Meta-Brain achieves 66.3% overall (12.7% gain over frozen) but only +1.5% over the universal LoRA baseline.\n      The routing weight matrix is weakly diagonal (0.58) — the router assigns some preference to the matched expert\n      but distributes substantial weight to others. This is not a routing failure (Phase 0 proves the signal exists)\n      but a correction-capacity failure: when experts produce similar outputs, the optimization has no incentive to\n      sharpen routing. The routing entropy (mean 0.89) is close to uniform (1.10), confirming near-flat routing.\n      The 3x parameter overhead of maintaining separate experts (6M vs 2M) yields only 1.5% marginal gain,\n      making the routing architecture difficult to justify on efficiency grounds alone.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle routing ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels. Measures upper bound\n      of routing-based improvement.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_overall: 67.8\n      oracle_vs_par_gap: 1.5\n      oracle_vs_universal_gap: 3.0\n      oracle_camera: 61.0\n      oracle_light: 70.0\n      oracle_background: 65.0\n      oracle_noise: 72.0\n      oracle_layout: 66.0\n      oracle_robot: 73.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle routing achieves 67.8% — only 3.0% above universal LoRA and 1.5% above learned PAR routing.\n      The small oracle-universal gap is the definitive evidence that the routing hypothesis is bottlenecked\n      by expert differentiation, not routing quality. Even with perfect perturbation-type information,\n      selecting the \"right\" rank-16 expert adds minimal value over the mixed-trained universal expert. The\n      oracle-PAR gap of 1.5% indicates PAR routing recovers roughly half (1.5/3.0) of the theoretically\n      available routing benefit, but the theoretical maximum itself is too small to matter. This ceiling\n      analysis shifts the research question from \"how to route better\" to \"how to make experts more different.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Oracle vs PAR vs Universal per type\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | Universal | PAR(MB) | Oracle | Δ(Oracle−Uni) | Δ(PAR−Uni) |\n          |------------|-----------|---------|--------|---------------|------------|\n          | Camera     | 57.0      | 59.0    | 61.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n          | Light      | 68.0      | 69.0    | 70.0   | +2.0          | +1.0       |\n          | Background | 61.0      | 63.0    | 65.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n          | Noise      | 70.0      | 71.0    | 72.0   | +2.0          | +1.0       |\n          | Layout     | 62.0      | 64.0    | 66.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n          | Robot      | 70.8      | 72.0    | 73.0   | +2.2          | +1.2       |\n          | **Overall**| **64.8**  |**66.3** |**67.8**| **+3.0**      | **+1.5**   |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Gap waterfall chart: Universal → PAR (+1.5) → Oracle (+1.5) → showing the theoretical ceiling is low and PAR already captures half of it.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The oracle ablation is the most important diagnostic in the worst tier. Oracle routing = 67.8% sets an\n      absolute ceiling for any routing-based approach with these rank-16 experts. The universal-to-oracle gap of\n      3.0% means perfect perturbation knowledge is worth at most 3 percentage points — a meaningful but modest gain\n      that ranks below the universal LoRA's own gain over frozen (11.2%). This proves the core routing hypothesis\n      fails not because of routing errors but because of insufficient expert differentiation at rank 16.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation ladder — 5-seed systematic comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Core evidence table: Frozen → Universal LoRA → Meta-Brain → Oracle, evaluated on LIBERO-Plus\n      with 5 seeds per condition. Statistical significance testing via paired t-test.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_mean: 53.6\n      frozen_std: 0.9\n      universal_mean: 64.8\n      universal_std: 1.1\n      meta_brain_mean: 66.3\n      meta_brain_std: 1.1\n      oracle_mean: 67.8\n      oracle_std: 1.0\n      gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.2\n      gain_frozen_to_meta_brain: 12.7\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 1.5\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain_std: 1.5\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain_pvalue: 0.12\n      gain_universal_to_oracle: 3.0\n      gain_universal_to_oracle_pvalue: 0.008\n      gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 1.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Five-seed evaluation reveals that Meta-Brain's +1.5% gain over universal LoRA is not statistically\n      significant (p=0.12, paired t-test). The seed-to-seed variance (std ~1.1%) is comparable to the\n      routing-attributed gain, meaning the benefit could be noise. In contrast, universal LoRA's +11.2%\n      gain over frozen IS significant (p<0.001), confirming that LoRA adaptation itself works — the routing\n      on top of it doesn't. Oracle-universal gap (3.0%, p=0.008) is significant but small in absolute terms.\n      The core story: most of the heavy lifting is done by LoRA adaptation (11.2/12.7 = 88% of total gain),\n      with routing contributing at most 12% of the improvement, not statistically distinguishable from zero.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Ablation ladder: Overall success rate (%, 5 seeds)\"\n        content: |\n          | Method         | Params | Seed1 | Seed2 | Seed3 | Seed4 | Seed5 | Mean  | Std  |\n          |----------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|------|\n          | Frozen pi0     | 0      | 52.4  | 54.8  | 53.0  | 54.2  | 53.6  | 53.6  | 0.9  |\n          | Universal LoRA | 2M     | 63.2  | 66.0  | 64.4  | 65.8  | 64.6  | 64.8  | 1.1  |\n          | Meta-Brain     | 6M     | 65.0  | 67.4  | 66.2  | 67.0  | 65.9  | 66.3  | 1.1  |\n          | Oracle routing | 6M     | 66.6  | 69.0  | 67.6  | 68.4  | 67.4  | 67.8  | 1.0  |\n      - title: \"Pairwise gains with significance\"\n        content: |\n          | Comparison                 | Δ Mean | Δ Std | p-value | Significant? |\n          |----------------------------|--------|-------|---------|-------------|\n          | Universal − Frozen         | +11.2  | 1.0   | <0.001  | Yes ***     |\n          | Meta-Brain − Frozen        | +12.7  | 1.0   | <0.001  | Yes ***     |\n          | Meta-Brain − Universal     | +1.5   | 1.5   | 0.12    | **No**      |\n          | Oracle − Universal         | +3.0   | 0.8   | 0.008   | Yes **      |\n          | Oracle − Meta-Brain        | +1.5   | 0.7   | 0.02    | Yes *       |\n      - title: \"Per-perturbation breakdown (mean over 5 seeds)\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | Frozen | Universal | Meta-Brain | Oracle | Δ(MB−Uni) |\n          |------------|--------|-----------|------------|--------|-----------|\n          | Camera     | 44.0   | 57.0      | 59.0       | 61.0   | +2.0      |\n          | Light      | 56.0   | 68.0      | 69.0       | 70.0   | +1.0      |\n          | Background | 49.0   | 61.0      | 63.0       | 65.0   | +2.0      |\n          | Noise      | 58.0   | 70.0      | 71.0       | 72.0   | +1.0      |\n          | Layout     | 52.0   | 62.0      | 64.0       | 66.0   | +2.0      |\n          | Robot      | 62.6   | 70.8      | 72.0       | 73.0   | +1.2      |\n          | **Overall**| **53.6**| **64.8** | **66.3**   |**67.8**| **+1.5**  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Ablation ladder bar chart with error bars (5 seeds): Frozen → Universal shows a large jump; Universal → Meta-Brain shows a thin, overlapping-CI sliver; Meta-Brain → Oracle shows another thin sliver. The visual immediately communicates that routing contributes negligible additional gain.\"\n      - \"Per-perturbation grouped bar chart (6 types × 4 methods): uniform small deltas across all types, no perturbation type shows dramatic routing benefit.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the central negative result. The ablation ladder shows a clear two-regime structure: (1) LoRA\n      adaptation produces a large, significant gain (+11.2%), and (2) routing on top of LoRA produces a small,\n      non-significant gain (+1.5%, p=0.12). The 95% CI for the Meta-Brain−Universal difference is [-0.5, +3.5],\n      which includes zero. Even the oracle ceiling is only +3.0% above universal, meaning no routing strategy\n      with these experts could achieve significance with 5 seeds. The per-perturbation breakdown shows\n      remarkably uniform small deltas (+1.0 to +2.0), suggesting no perturbation type is a \"routing winner.\"\n      This pattern is consistent with the hypothesis that rank-16 LoRA convergence, not routing quality,\n      is the binding constraint.\n\n  - name: \"FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      Reproduce FTM on pi0 for visual perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain on visual\n      perturbations. Establish Meta-Brain's value proposition beyond visual-only methods.\n    expected_results:\n      ftm_camera: 63.0\n      ftm_light: 71.0\n      ftm_background: 62.0\n      ftm_noise: 69.0\n      ftm_visual_avg: 66.2\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 67.0\n      meta_brain_visual_avg: 65.5\n      universal_visual_avg: 64.0\n      ftm_on_layout: null\n      ftm_on_robot: null\n      meta_brain_overall_advantage: 1.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      FTM achieves 66.2% on visual perturbations with only ~4K trainable parameters, which is competitive\n      with LoRA-V (67.0%) and actually exceeds Meta-Brain's visual performance (65.5%). FTM's architecture\n      (feature transformation modules) is specifically optimized for visual distribution shifts, giving it\n      an edge on camera/light perturbations. Meta-Brain's soft routing dilutes visual expert weight across\n      all three experts (routing weight 0.52 for LoRA-V on visual inputs), so it underperforms the dedicated\n      LoRA-V. Meta-Brain's only advantage is coverage of spatial/physical types, but with the +1.5% overall\n      gain over universal being non-significant, the coverage argument is weak. In worst tier, FTM's\n      parameter efficiency on visual perturbations (14.5% gain / 4K params) dramatically outperforms\n      Meta-Brain's overall efficiency.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Visual perturbation comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Method      | Params  | Camera | Light | BG   | Noise | Vis Avg |\n          |-------------|---------|--------|-------|------|-------|---------|\n          | Frozen pi0  | 0       | 44.0   | 56.0  | 49.0 | 58.0  | 51.8   |\n          | FTM         | 4K      | 63.0   | 71.0  | 62.0 | 69.0  | 66.2   |\n          | Universal   | 2M      | 57.0   | 68.0  | 61.0 | 70.0  | 64.0   |\n          | LoRA-V      | 2M      | 61.0   | 70.0  | 65.0 | 72.0  | 67.0   |\n          | Meta-Brain  | 6M      | 59.0   | 69.0  | 63.0 | 71.0  | 65.5   |\n      - title: \"Coverage comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Method     | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | Coverage |\n          |------------|--------|---------|----------|---------|----------|\n          | FTM        | 66.2   | —       | —        | —       | Partial  |\n          | Meta-Brain | 65.5   | 64.0    | 72.0     | 66.3    | Full     |\n          | Universal  | 64.0   | 62.0    | 70.8     | 64.8    | Full     |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Visual-perturbation-only bar chart: FTM vs LoRA-V vs Meta-Brain vs Universal. FTM is competitive or better than Meta-Brain on all visual types despite 1500x fewer parameters.\"\n    analysis: >\n      FTM comparison is damaging to Meta-Brain's narrative in worst tier. FTM achieves comparable visual\n      robustness with 4K params (66.2% visual avg) while Meta-Brain requires 6M params for 65.5% on visual\n      types. Meta-Brain's theoretical advantage — full perturbation coverage — yields only +1.5% overall\n      gain over universal. A reviewer could argue: \"just use FTM for visual + universal LoRA for the rest\"\n      as a simpler, more parameter-efficient alternative. In the pivot framing, this comparison supports\n      the design-dimension analysis narrative: different adaptation strategies (feature transformation vs\n      LoRA routing) have different efficiency profiles, and the optimal strategy depends on the perturbation\n      distribution rather than routing quality.\n\n  - name: \"Parameter efficiency analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Scatter plot comparison\n      across all methods including cross-paper baselines.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 12.7\n      meta_brain_params_M: 6.0\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.12\n      universal_gain_over_frozen: 11.2\n      universal_params_M: 2.0\n      universal_gain_per_M: 5.60\n      ftm_gain_over_frozen_visual: 14.4\n      ftm_params_M: 0.004\n      ftm_gain_per_M_visual: 3600.0\n      vla_gse_gain_over_lora: 12.0\n      vla_gse_params_M: 114.0\n      vla_gse_gain_per_M_over_lora: 0.105\n      meta_brain_gain_over_lora: 1.5\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M_over_lora: 0.25\n    result_rationale: >\n      Parameter efficiency analysis is unfavorable for Meta-Brain in worst tier. Universal LoRA achieves\n      5.60% gain per million parameters — 2.6x more efficient than Meta-Brain (2.12). The 3x parameter\n      budget (6M vs 2M) buys only 1.5% additional gain. FTM dominates on visual-only efficiency\n      (3600 gain/M on visual). When normalized to gain-over-LoRA-baseline (the routing-attributable\n      improvement), Meta-Brain's efficiency drops to 0.25%/M, meaning most of its gain comes from the\n      LoRA components themselves, not the routing mechanism. The scatter plot will show Meta-Brain sitting\n      in an unfavorable region: more parameters than universal LoRA for negligible additional gain, and\n      far from FTM's visual-only efficiency frontier.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Parameter efficiency comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Method         | Backbone   | Params  | Gain/Frozen | Gain/M  | Coverage |\n          |----------------|------------|---------|-------------|---------|----------|\n          | FTM            | pi0        | 4K      | +14.4 (vis) | 3600.0  | Visual   |\n          | Universal LoRA | pi0        | 2M      | +11.2       | 5.60    | Full     |\n          | Meta-Brain     | pi0        | 6M      | +12.7       | 2.12    | Full     |\n          | VLA-GSE*       | Qwen3-VL   | 114M    | +27.6†      | 0.24    | Full     |\n          * different backbone  † estimated gain over Qwen3-VL frozen baseline\n      - title: \"Routing-attributable efficiency\"\n        content: |\n          | Method     | Gain over Uni LoRA | Routing params | Gain/M (routing) |\n          |------------|--------------------|----------------|------------------|\n          | Meta-Brain | +1.5               | 4M (extra)     | 0.375            |\n          | VLA-GSE*   | +12.0              | 114M           | 0.105            |\n          * cross-backbone, not directly comparable\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Log-scale scatter plot: x=trainable params, y=gain-over-frozen. Universal LoRA sits on a better efficiency frontier than Meta-Brain. FTM (visual-only) dominates the low-param region. Meta-Brain is right-shifted from Universal with minimal y-axis gain.\"\n      - \"Marginal efficiency bar chart: gain per additional M params spent beyond universal LoRA. Meta-Brain's marginal efficiency (0.375%/M) is shown as negligible.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The parameter efficiency analysis is the quantitative nail in the routing hypothesis coffin. Universal\n      LoRA achieves 88% of Meta-Brain's total gain (11.2/12.7) with 33% of the parameters (2M/6M). The\n      additional 4M parameters allocated to maintaining separate experts + router yield 0.375% gain per M\n      — a poor return. In the pivot framing, this supports the design-dimension analysis: parameter-matched\n      universal adaptation is a strong default, and routing-based approaches need either higher-capacity\n      experts (rank 32+) or compositional perturbation scenarios to justify the overhead.\n\n  - name: \"Correction Memory ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM adds routing-weight\n      modulation from retrieved exemplars via FAISS.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_no_cm: 66.3\n      meta_brain_with_cm: 66.7\n      cm_ablation_delta: 0.4\n      cm_delta_camera: 0.6\n      cm_delta_light: 0.2\n      cm_delta_background: 0.8\n      cm_delta_noise: 0.0\n      cm_delta_layout: 0.4\n      cm_delta_robot: 0.2\n      cm_pvalue: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      CM adds 0.4% overall — well within noise (p=0.38). Since the base routing weights are already near-uniform\n      (diagonality 0.58), modulating them with retrieved exemplars has minimal effect: modulating a near-flat\n      distribution stays near-flat. CM's theoretical value is sharpening routing confidence for ambiguous cases,\n      but when the experts themselves produce similar outputs, sharper routing does not translate to better\n      correction. The per-type breakdown shows CM's largest effect on background (+0.8%), which is the type with\n      highest cross-expert variance in plan_002, but even this is not significant. CM adds one learned parameter\n      (alpha) and CPU-bound FAISS retrieval — the complexity-to-gain ratio is extremely unfavorable.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"CM ablation per perturbation type\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | No CM  | With CM | Δ    |\n          |------------|--------|---------|------|\n          | Camera     | 59.0   | 59.6    | +0.6 |\n          | Light      | 69.0   | 69.2    | +0.2 |\n          | Background | 63.0   | 63.8    | +0.8 |\n          | Noise      | 71.0   | 71.0    | +0.0 |\n          | Layout     | 64.0   | 64.4    | +0.4 |\n          | Robot      | 72.0   | 72.2    | +0.2 |\n          | **Overall**| **66.3**|**66.7**| **+0.4** |\n          p = 0.38 (paired t-test, not significant)\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Paired bar chart (with/without CM): differences are invisible at chart scale, visually reinforcing the null result.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CM is a clear null result in worst tier. Present as optional module with honest reporting: +0.4%\n      (p=0.38). In the pivot framing, this supports the broader conclusion that the routing bottleneck is\n      expert capacity, not routing precision. Even augmenting routing with episodic memory (retrieved\n      exemplar context) cannot overcome the fundamental limitation that rank-16 experts produce similar outputs.\n      Recommend presenting CM as a \"future work\" direction for higher-capacity expert settings rather than\n      a contribution of this paper.\n\n  - name: \"Router architecture comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP, linear classifier, attention-based router.\n      All on PaliGemma layer 6. Measure routing accuracy and downstream task success.\n    expected_results:\n      mlp_routing_accuracy: 99.3\n      mlp_downstream: 66.3\n      linear_routing_accuracy: 98.1\n      linear_downstream: 66.0\n      attention_routing_accuracy: 99.1\n      attention_downstream: 66.2\n      downstream_range: 0.3\n      routing_accuracy_range: 1.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      All three router architectures produce nearly identical downstream performance (66.0–66.3%, range 0.3%)\n      despite varying routing accuracy (98.1–99.3%). This confirms that routing quality is not the bottleneck:\n      even the simplest linear classifier (98.1% accuracy) achieves 66.0%, only 0.3% below the best MLP router.\n      The attention-based router, despite being more expressive, cannot extract additional routing value because\n      the experts themselves are too similar. The routing accuracy → downstream transfer coefficient is near-zero:\n      a 1.2% improvement in routing accuracy translates to at most 0.3% downstream gain. This dissociation\n      between routing accuracy and downstream success is the strongest evidence that the correction-capacity\n      bottleneck, not routing architecture, limits Meta-Brain's effectiveness.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Router architecture comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Router     | Params (router) | Route Acc (%) | Downstream (%) | Δ from MLP |\n          |------------|-----------------|---------------|-----------------|------------|\n          | Linear     | 6K              | 98.1          | 66.0            | −0.3       |\n          | MLP (2-layer) | 33K          | 99.3          | 66.3            | —          |\n          | Attention  | 132K            | 99.1          | 66.2            | −0.1       |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-axis chart: routing accuracy (left y-axis) vs downstream success (right y-axis) for 3 router types. Routing accuracy varies more than downstream success, showing the disconnect.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Router architecture is irrelevant in worst tier. All variants perform within 0.3% of each other\n      downstream, regardless of routing accuracy differences. This is useful for the design-dimension analysis:\n      it provides evidence that perturbation-diagnostic routing is easy (even a linear classifier suffices) but\n      that the downstream value of routing is limited by expert capacity. Recommend using the linear router in\n      the paper (simplest, nearly identical performance) and framing the comparison as evidence for the\n      \"routing is easy, correction is hard\" narrative.\n\n  - name: \"6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (6 LoRA experts, rank 8 each) vs 3-class coarse routing\n      (3 LoRA experts, rank 16 each) under matched total parameter budget (~6M).\n    expected_results:\n      three_class_overall: 66.3\n      six_class_overall: 65.8\n      six_class_delta: -0.5\n      six_class_camera: 59.4\n      six_class_light: 69.2\n      six_class_background: 62.4\n      six_class_noise: 70.0\n      six_class_layout: 63.6\n      six_class_robot: 70.2\n      six_class_routing_accuracy: 98.8\n      six_class_routing_diagonality: 0.51\n      marginal_gain_per_expert: -0.08\n    result_rationale: >\n      6-class routing performs slightly worse than 3-class (65.8 vs 66.3, Δ=−0.5%). Under a fixed parameter\n      budget, doubling the number of experts halves each expert's rank (rank 8 vs rank 16), reducing individual\n      expert capacity. The finer routing granularity cannot compensate for the reduced per-expert correction\n      power. Additionally, 6-class routing diagonality (0.51) is lower than 3-class (0.58) — with more classes,\n      the routing weights spread more thinly, approaching uniform distribution. The intra-Visual perturbations\n      (camera, light, background, noise) occupy overlapping representation spaces in PaliGemma layer 6\n      (consistent with Phase 0's 6-class accuracy being only marginally above 3-class), so splitting them into\n      separate experts creates artificial boundaries in a continuous representation space. This result validates\n      the 3-class taxonomy and argues against finer granularity at this parameter budget.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"6-class vs 3-class comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Config  | Experts | Rank/Expert | Total Params | Overall | Diag  |\n          |---------|---------|-------------|-------------|---------|-------|\n          | 3-class | 3       | 16          | ~6M         | 66.3    | 0.58  |\n          | 6-class | 6       | 8           | ~6M         | 65.8    | 0.51  |\n          | Δ       |         |             |             | −0.5    | −0.07 |\n      - title: \"6-class per-perturbation results\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | 3-class | 6-class | Δ    |\n          |------------|---------|---------|------|\n          | Camera     | 59.0    | 59.4    | +0.4 |\n          | Light      | 69.0    | 69.2    | +0.2 |\n          | Background | 63.0    | 62.4    | −0.6 |\n          | Noise      | 71.0    | 70.0    | −1.0 |\n          | Layout     | 64.0    | 63.6    | −0.4 |\n          | Robot      | 72.0    | 70.2    | −1.8 |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side routing heatmaps: 3×3 (3-class) vs 6×6 (6-class). 6-class shows more diffuse routing weights with weaker diagonal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Finer granularity hurts under fixed parameter budget. Camera and light benefit marginally (+0.4, +0.2)\n      from dedicated experts, but robot (−1.8) and noise (−1.0) degrade substantially — their rank-8 experts\n      lack capacity for effective correction. This is a capacity-granularity tradeoff: more routing precision ×\n      less correction capacity = net negative. Addresses reviewer concern C5 directly: 3-class is not too coarse;\n      it is actually the better operating point given the rank-16 constraint. Finer granularity would require\n      proportionally higher total rank budget.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on SmolVLA to test generalizability of\n      perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\n    expected_results:\n      smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.971\n      smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n      smolvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.943\n      smolvla_frozen_baseline: 48.2\n      smolvla_universal_lora: 58.6\n      smolvla_meta_brain: 59.8\n      smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 11.6\n      smolvla_gain_over_universal: 1.2\n      smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.54\n    result_rationale: >\n      Phase 0 replicates strongly on SmolVLA (3-class F1=0.971, K=1 F1=0.943) — perturbation type is\n      diagnosable from frozen activations across architectures. This is the central positive finding for the\n      pivot narrative: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type as a general property, not a\n      pi0 artifact. Phase 1 shows the same pattern as pi0: large universal LoRA gain (+10.4 over frozen),\n      marginal routing gain (+1.2 over universal, not significant). SmolVLA's lower absolute performance\n      (48.2% frozen, 59.8% Meta-Brain) reflects its smaller backbone capacity, but the relative pattern\n      is identical — LoRA adaptation helps, routing doesn't add much. Routing diagonality (0.54) is even\n      weaker than pi0 (0.58), consistent with SmolVLA's slightly lower Phase 0 accuracy. Cross-backbone\n      replication strengthens Phase 0 as the lead contribution and confirms that the routing limitation\n      is a property of the rank-16 LoRA approach, not a backbone-specific artifact.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-backbone Phase 0 comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Metric            | pi0 (PaliGemma) | SmolVLA      |\n          |-------------------|-----------------|--------------|\n          | 3-class F1        | 0.993           | 0.971        |\n          | Best layer        | 6               | 4            |\n          | K=1 F1            | 1.000           | 0.943        |\n          | Binary AUC        | 1.000           | 0.998        |\n      - title: \"Cross-backbone Phase 1 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Method         | pi0   | SmolVLA | Pattern match? |\n          |----------------|-------|---------|----------------|\n          | Frozen         | 53.6  | 48.2    | —              |\n          | Universal LoRA | 64.8  | 58.6    | —              |\n          | Meta-Brain     | 66.3  | 59.8    | —              |\n          | Δ(MB−Uni)      | +1.5  | +1.2    | ✓ same pattern |\n          | Routing diag   | 0.58  | 0.54    | ✓ same pattern |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"t-SNE comparison: pi0 layer 6 vs SmolVLA layer 4 activations, both showing clear 3-class clustering. Visual evidence that perturbation diagnosability is architecture-general.\"\n      - \"Mirror ablation ladder: pi0 (left) vs SmolVLA (right) with matching relative patterns despite different absolute baselines.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-backbone replication is the strongest evidence for the pivot paper. Phase 0 generalizes (F1>0.97\n      on SmolVLA) while Phase 1's routing limitation also generalizes (Δ=+1.2, not significant). This\n      supports the reframed contribution: \"VLA representations universally encode perturbation type\" (positive\n      finding, lead contribution) + \"diagnostic routing yields marginal downstream gains at rank-16\"\n      (negative-but-informative, generalizes across backbones). SmolVLA's different architecture (different\n      vision encoder, different layer structure) makes the Phase 0 replication a genuine generalization result,\n      not a trivial confirmation. Addresses reviewer concern C3 directly.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA layer placement ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: early (2-6), mid (6-12), late (12-16), all layers.\n      Test whether type-specific layer placement improves specialization.\n    expected_results:\n      early_layers_overall: 65.8\n      mid_layers_overall: 66.1\n      late_layers_overall: 65.4\n      all_layers_overall: 66.5\n      early_visual_advantage: 0.6\n      mid_spatial_advantage: 0.4\n      late_physical_advantage: 0.2\n      optimal_vs_default_gap: 0.2\n      visual_lora_best_placement: \"early (2-6)\"\n      spatial_lora_best_placement: \"mid (6-12)\"\n      physical_lora_best_placement: \"all\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Layer placement produces negligible differences (65.4–66.5%, range 1.1%). The hypothesis that Visual LoRA\n      benefits from early layers, Spatial from mid, and Physical from late receives only weak support: Visual\n      expert on early layers gains +0.6% on visual types (compared to default all-layer), Spatial on mid gains\n      +0.4% on layout, but Physical on late layers actually degrades (−0.8% on robot). The \"all layers\" placement\n      (66.5%) is marginally best overall — spreading LoRA across all layers provides the most general correction.\n      At rank 16, the LoRA correction is too low-rank to benefit from layer-specific placement: the correction\n      capacity is distributed thinly regardless of which layers are targeted. The type-specific placement\n      hypothesis would require higher-rank experts where layer-specific corrections could diverge more\n      substantially. This ablation provides mild evidence for the \"experts converge to similar solutions\"\n      hypothesis — even varying their layer targets doesn't produce differentiated behaviors.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Layer placement overall results\"\n        content: |\n          | Placement    | Layers  | Overall | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\n          |--------------|---------|---------|--------|---------|----------|\n          | Early        | 2-6     | 65.8    | 66.1   | 63.0    | 71.0     |\n          | Mid          | 6-12    | 66.1    | 65.4   | 64.8    | 72.2     |\n          | Late         | 12-16   | 65.4    | 64.8   | 63.2    | 71.8     |\n          | All (default)| 2-16    | 66.5    | 65.5   | 64.0    | 72.0     |\n          | Range        |         | 1.1     | 1.3    | 1.8     | 1.2      |\n      - title: \"Type-specific placement hypothesis test\"\n        content: |\n          | Expert  | Hypothesis        | Best placement | Δ vs all-layers | Confirmed? |\n          |---------|-------------------|----------------|-----------------|------------|\n          | LoRA-V  | Early best        | Early (+0.6)   | +0.6            | Weak       |\n          | LoRA-S  | Mid best          | Mid (+0.8)     | +0.8            | Weak       |\n          | LoRA-P  | Late best         | All (−0.8 late)| −0.8            | No         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap: (layer placement × perturbation type) success rates. Shows flat landscape with no strong type-layer interaction.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Layer placement is a near-null result. The 1.1% overall range is within seed-level variance, and no\n      type-layer pairing produces a compelling advantage. Physical expert on late layers is actually worse,\n      contradicting the mechanistic hypothesis. At rank 16, the LoRA updates are too constrained to develop\n      layer-specific correction strategies. This supports the overall worst-tier narrative: the rank-16\n      LoRA bottleneck prevents meaningful specialization regardless of routing quality, expert assignment,\n      or layer targeting. Present as supporting evidence for the \"capacity is the bottleneck\" conclusion\n      and a clear direction for future work (higher-rank experts + layer-aware placement).\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11382,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11382},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7b30f17-3885-4360-950b-9073de7caf33","uuid":"72124505-4f8b-4a73-b73e-90ae4f1ebae1"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":403868,"duration_api_ms":405081,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: worst\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Paper pivots from 'routing improves correction' to 'what VLA representations reveal about perturbation processing'.\n  Lead contribution becomes Phase 0 mechanistic discovery (F1=1.0 at K=1 single layer). Contribution 2 becomes\n  a systematic negative-but-informative result: explicit perturbation-diagnostic routing yields only marginal,\n  statistically non-significant gains over implicit mixed-perturbation LoRA training, providing evidence that\n  rank-16 LoRA mixed training already captures perturbation-conditional structure. Universal LoRA is the\n  surprising finding — it implicitly learns type-aware correction without any routing signal. Title shifts to\n  'Do VLAs Know What Went Wrong? Probing Perturbation Representations and the Limits of Diagnostic Routing'.\n  Key framing: the routing signal is real (Phase 0 proves it), but the correction bottleneck is expert capacity,\n  not routing quality. This reframes the contribution as a design-dimension analysis that maps out the landscape\n  for future work with higher-rank experts, multi-backbone settings, or compositional perturbation scenarios.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n\n  - name: \"LoRA expert training — 3 specialized + 1 universal\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      Train 4 LoRA adapters (rank 16) on frozen pi0 PaliGemma: LoRA-V (Visual), LoRA-S (Spatial),\n      LoRA-P (Physical), Universal (all types mixed). Evaluate each on all perturbation types.\n    expected_results:\n      lora_v_on_camera: 61.0\n      lora_v_on_light: 70.0\n      lora_v_on_background: 65.0\n      lora_v_on_sensor_noise: 72.0\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 67.0\n      lora_s_on_layout: 66.0\n      lora_p_on_robot: 73.0\n      universal_on_camera: 57.0\n      universal_on_light: 68.0\n      universal_on_background: 61.0\n      universal_on_sensor_noise: 70.0\n      universal_on_layout: 62.0\n      universal_on_robot: 70.8\n      universal_overall: 64.8\n      max_specialization_delta: 4.0\n      lora_v_on_layout_cross: 58.0\n      lora_v_on_robot_cross: 65.0\n      lora_s_on_visual_avg_cross: 59.5\n      lora_p_on_visual_avg_cross: 60.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Specialized experts show only marginal gains over universal on their own types (max +4.0% on\n      background/layout, +2.0–2.2% on light/noise/robot). Root cause: rank-16 LoRA has limited correction\n      capacity (~2M params per expert), and at this rank the parameter space is too constrained to learn\n      meaningfully different type-specific behaviors. All experts converge to similar low-rank corrections\n      that primarily recover the frozen backbone's general robustness gap. The universal LoRA, trained on\n      mixed perturbation data, implicitly learns a perturbation-conditional representation — mixed training\n      acts as a form of implicit multi-task learning that covers the type-specific corrections nearly as well\n      as explicit specialization. Cross-type degradation exists (LoRA-V on layout drops to 58.0 vs universal 62.0)\n      but is moderate, further indicating that experts are not highly differentiated.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-evaluation matrix: Expert × Perturbation Type (success rate %)\"\n        content: |\n          | Expert      | Camera | Light | BG   | Noise | Layout | Robot | Avg   |\n          |-------------|--------|-------|------|-------|--------|-------|-------|\n          | LoRA-V      | **61.0** | **70.0** | **65.0** | **72.0** | 58.0 | 65.0 | 65.2 |\n          | LoRA-S      | 53.0   | 63.0  | 57.0 | 65.0  | **66.0** | 67.0 | 61.8 |\n          | LoRA-P      | 54.0   | 64.0  | 58.0 | 66.0  | 60.0   | **73.0** | 62.5 |\n          | Universal   | 57.0   | 68.0  | 61.0 | 70.0  | 62.0   | 70.8  | 64.8 |\n          | Frozen pi0  | 44.0   | 56.0  | 49.0 | 58.0  | 52.0   | 62.6  | 53.6 |\n          Bold = expert matched to own perturbation type.\n      - title: \"Specialization delta: Specialized − Universal per type\"\n        content: |\n          | Type        | Specialized | Universal | Delta |\n          |-------------|-------------|-----------|-------|\n          | Camera      | 61.0        | 57.0      | +4.0  |\n          | Light       | 70.0        | 68.0      | +2.0  |\n          | Background  | 65.0        | 61.0      | +4.0  |\n          | Noise       | 72.0        | 70.0      | +2.0  |\n          | Layout      | 66.0        | 62.0      | +4.0  |\n          | Robot       | 73.0        | 70.8      | +2.2  |\n          | Max delta   |             |           | 4.0   |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Grouped bar chart: per-perturbation success rate for each expert + universal. Shows experts are only slightly above universal on matched types and below on mismatched types.\"\n      - \"Heatmap of (Expert × Type) deltas relative to universal, highlighting the narrow specialization corridor.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The cross-evaluation matrix reveals that rank-16 LoRA experts converge to similar correction strategies.\n      Universal LoRA averages 64.8% overall, while matched experts average 67.8% on their own types — a\n      difference of only 3.0%. Critically, mismatched experts often underperform universal (LoRA-V on layout:\n      58.0 vs 62.0; LoRA-S on visual avg: 59.5 vs 64.0), meaning routing must be accurate just to avoid\n      degradation. This creates a narrow \"routing benefit window\" where the expert-universal delta (~2–4%) must\n      exceed the routing-error cost. At rank-16, the expert capacity bottleneck limits the maximum extractable\n      benefit from routing, regardless of routing quality.\n\n  - name: \"Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      Assemble PAR router (layer 6 MLP) with 3 LoRA experts under soft routing. End-to-end routing\n      fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters. Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus.\n    expected_results:\n      libero_plus_overall: 66.3\n      gain_over_frozen: 12.7\n      gain_over_universal: 1.5\n      per_type_camera: 59.0\n      per_type_light: 69.0\n      per_type_background: 63.0\n      per_type_noise: 71.0\n      per_type_layout: 64.0\n      per_type_robot: 72.0\n      routing_diagonality: 0.58\n      routing_weight_visual_on_visual: 0.52\n      routing_weight_spatial_on_spatial: 0.61\n      routing_weight_physical_on_physical: 0.63\n      routing_entropy_mean: 0.89\n    result_rationale: >\n      Soft routing weights are near-uniform (diagonality 0.58, mean entropy 0.89 vs max 1.10 for 3-class\n      uniform). The PAR router correctly classifies perturbation type (Phase 0 F1=0.993) but the learned\n      routing-to-correction mapping during fine-tuning converges toward distributing weight across all experts\n      rather than concentrating on the matched one. This happens because the expert outputs are too similar\n      (rank-16 convergence from plan_002) — the optimization landscape has no strong gradient toward diagonal\n      routing since all experts provide nearly equal correction. The resulting Meta-Brain captures most of the\n      universal LoRA gain (12.7 vs 11.2 over frozen) but adds only 1.5% from routing, which is within the\n      noise floor of 5-seed evaluation.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"PAR routing weight matrix (rows = true type, columns = expert weight)\"\n        content: |\n          | True Type | w(LoRA-V) | w(LoRA-S) | w(LoRA-P) | Entropy |\n          |-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n          | Visual    | **0.52**  | 0.24      | 0.24      | 0.94    |\n          | Spatial   | 0.21      | **0.61**  | 0.18      | 0.83    |\n          | Physical  | 0.19      | 0.18      | **0.63**  | 0.80    |\n          | Diag avg  |           |           |           | 0.58    |\n          Perfect diagonality = 1.0, uniform = 0.33. Observed 0.58 = weak preference.\n      - title: \"Meta-Brain per-perturbation results\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation | Frozen | Universal | Meta-Brain | Δ(MB−Uni) |\n          |--------------|--------|-----------|------------|-----------|\n          | Camera       | 44.0   | 57.0      | 59.0       | +2.0      |\n          | Light        | 56.0   | 68.0      | 69.0       | +1.0      |\n          | Background   | 49.0   | 61.0      | 63.0       | +2.0      |\n          | Noise        | 58.0   | 70.0      | 71.0       | +1.0      |\n          | Layout       | 52.0   | 62.0      | 64.0       | +2.0      |\n          | Robot        | 62.6   | 70.8      | 72.0       | +1.2      |\n          | **Overall**  | **53.6** | **64.8** | **66.3**   | **+1.5**  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Routing weight heatmap (3×3): near-uniform rather than diagonal, with max diagonal entry only 0.63.\"\n      - \"Per-perturbation stacked bar: Frozen → Universal gain (large) vs Universal → Meta-Brain gain (thin sliver) visually demonstrates marginal routing benefit.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Meta-Brain achieves 66.3% overall (12.7% gain over frozen) but only +1.5% over the universal LoRA baseline.\n      The routing weight matrix is weakly diagonal (0.58) — the router assigns some preference to the matched expert\n      but distributes substantial weight to others. This is not a routing failure (Phase 0 proves the signal exists)\n      but a correction-capacity failure: when experts produce similar outputs, the optimization has no incentive to\n      sharpen routing. The routing entropy (mean 0.89) is close to uniform (1.10), confirming near-flat routing.\n      The 3x parameter overhead of maintaining separate experts (6M vs 2M) yields only 1.5% marginal gain,\n      making the routing architecture difficult to justify on efficiency grounds alone.\n\n  - name: \"Oracle routing ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels. Measures upper bound\n      of routing-based improvement.\n    expected_results:\n      oracle_overall: 67.8\n      oracle_vs_par_gap: 1.5\n      oracle_vs_universal_gap: 3.0\n      oracle_camera: 61.0\n      oracle_light: 70.0\n      oracle_background: 65.0\n      oracle_noise: 72.0\n      oracle_layout: 66.0\n      oracle_robot: 73.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle routing achieves 67.8% — only 3.0% above universal LoRA and 1.5% above learned PAR routing.\n      The small oracle-universal gap is the definitive evidence that the routing hypothesis is bottlenecked\n      by expert differentiation, not routing quality. Even with perfect perturbation-type information,\n      selecting the \"right\" rank-16 expert adds minimal value over the mixed-trained universal expert. The\n      oracle-PAR gap of 1.5% indicates PAR routing recovers roughly half (1.5/3.0) of the theoretically\n      available routing benefit, but the theoretical maximum itself is too small to matter. This ceiling\n      analysis shifts the research question from \"how to route better\" to \"how to make experts more different.\"\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Oracle vs PAR vs Universal per type\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | Universal | PAR(MB) | Oracle | Δ(Oracle−Uni) | Δ(PAR−Uni) |\n          |------------|-----------|---------|--------|---------------|------------|\n          | Camera     | 57.0      | 59.0    | 61.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n          | Light      | 68.0      | 69.0    | 70.0   | +2.0          | +1.0       |\n          | Background | 61.0      | 63.0    | 65.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n          | Noise      | 70.0      | 71.0    | 72.0   | +2.0          | +1.0       |\n          | Layout     | 62.0      | 64.0    | 66.0   | +4.0          | +2.0       |\n          | Robot      | 70.8      | 72.0    | 73.0   | +2.2          | +1.2       |\n          | **Overall**| **64.8**  |**66.3** |**67.8**| **+3.0**      | **+1.5**   |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Gap waterfall chart: Universal → PAR (+1.5) → Oracle (+1.5) → showing the theoretical ceiling is low and PAR already captures half of it.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The oracle ablation is the most important diagnostic in the worst tier. Oracle routing = 67.8% sets an\n      absolute ceiling for any routing-based approach with these rank-16 experts. The universal-to-oracle gap of\n      3.0% means perfect perturbation knowledge is worth at most 3 percentage points — a meaningful but modest gain\n      that ranks below the universal LoRA's own gain over frozen (11.2%). This proves the core routing hypothesis\n      fails not because of routing errors but because of insufficient expert differentiation at rank 16.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation ladder — 5-seed systematic comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Core evidence table: Frozen → Universal LoRA → Meta-Brain → Oracle, evaluated on LIBERO-Plus\n      with 5 seeds per condition. Statistical significance testing via paired t-test.\n    expected_results:\n      frozen_mean: 53.6\n      frozen_std: 0.9\n      universal_mean: 64.8\n      universal_std: 1.1\n      meta_brain_mean: 66.3\n      meta_brain_std: 1.1\n      oracle_mean: 67.8\n      oracle_std: 1.0\n      gain_frozen_to_universal: 11.2\n      gain_frozen_to_meta_brain: 12.7\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain: 1.5\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain_std: 1.5\n      gain_universal_to_meta_brain_pvalue: 0.12\n      gain_universal_to_oracle: 3.0\n      gain_universal_to_oracle_pvalue: 0.008\n      gain_meta_brain_to_oracle: 1.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Five-seed evaluation reveals that Meta-Brain's +1.5% gain over universal LoRA is not statistically\n      significant (p=0.12, paired t-test). The seed-to-seed variance (std ~1.1%) is comparable to the\n      routing-attributed gain, meaning the benefit could be noise. In contrast, universal LoRA's +11.2%\n      gain over frozen IS significant (p<0.001), confirming that LoRA adaptation itself works — the routing\n      on top of it doesn't. Oracle-universal gap (3.0%, p=0.008) is significant but small in absolute terms.\n      The core story: most of the heavy lifting is done by LoRA adaptation (11.2/12.7 = 88% of total gain),\n      with routing contributing at most 12% of the improvement, not statistically distinguishable from zero.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Ablation ladder: Overall success rate (%, 5 seeds)\"\n        content: |\n          | Method         | Params | Seed1 | Seed2 | Seed3 | Seed4 | Seed5 | Mean  | Std  |\n          |----------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|-------|------|\n          | Frozen pi0     | 0      | 52.4  | 54.8  | 53.0  | 54.2  | 53.6  | 53.6  | 0.9  |\n          | Universal LoRA | 2M     | 63.2  | 66.0  | 64.4  | 65.8  | 64.6  | 64.8  | 1.1  |\n          | Meta-Brain     | 6M     | 65.0  | 67.4  | 66.2  | 67.0  | 65.9  | 66.3  | 1.1  |\n          | Oracle routing | 6M     | 66.6  | 69.0  | 67.6  | 68.4  | 67.4  | 67.8  | 1.0  |\n      - title: \"Pairwise gains with significance\"\n        content: |\n          | Comparison                 | Δ Mean | Δ Std | p-value | Significant? |\n          |----------------------------|--------|-------|---------|-------------|\n          | Universal − Frozen         | +11.2  | 1.0   | <0.001  | Yes ***     |\n          | Meta-Brain − Frozen        | +12.7  | 1.0   | <0.001  | Yes ***     |\n          | Meta-Brain − Universal     | +1.5   | 1.5   | 0.12    | **No**      |\n          | Oracle − Universal         | +3.0   | 0.8   | 0.008   | Yes **      |\n          | Oracle − Meta-Brain        | +1.5   | 0.7   | 0.02    | Yes *       |\n      - title: \"Per-perturbation breakdown (mean over 5 seeds)\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | Frozen | Universal | Meta-Brain | Oracle | Δ(MB−Uni) |\n          |------------|--------|-----------|------------|--------|-----------|\n          | Camera     | 44.0   | 57.0      | 59.0       | 61.0   | +2.0      |\n          | Light      | 56.0   | 68.0      | 69.0       | 70.0   | +1.0      |\n          | Background | 49.0   | 61.0      | 63.0       | 65.0   | +2.0      |\n          | Noise      | 58.0   | 70.0      | 71.0       | 72.0   | +1.0      |\n          | Layout     | 52.0   | 62.0      | 64.0       | 66.0   | +2.0      |\n          | Robot      | 62.6   | 70.8      | 72.0       | 73.0   | +1.2      |\n          | **Overall**| **53.6**| **64.8** | **66.3**   |**67.8**| **+1.5**  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Ablation ladder bar chart with error bars (5 seeds): Frozen → Universal shows a large jump; Universal → Meta-Brain shows a thin, overlapping-CI sliver; Meta-Brain → Oracle shows another thin sliver. The visual immediately communicates that routing contributes negligible additional gain.\"\n      - \"Per-perturbation grouped bar chart (6 types × 4 methods): uniform small deltas across all types, no perturbation type shows dramatic routing benefit.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the central negative result. The ablation ladder shows a clear two-regime structure: (1) LoRA\n      adaptation produces a large, significant gain (+11.2%), and (2) routing on top of LoRA produces a small,\n      non-significant gain (+1.5%, p=0.12). The 95% CI for the Meta-Brain−Universal difference is [-0.5, +3.5],\n      which includes zero. Even the oracle ceiling is only +3.0% above universal, meaning no routing strategy\n      with these experts could achieve significance with 5 seeds. The per-perturbation breakdown shows\n      remarkably uniform small deltas (+1.0 to +2.0), suggesting no perturbation type is a \"routing winner.\"\n      This pattern is consistent with the hypothesis that rank-16 LoRA convergence, not routing quality,\n      is the binding constraint.\n\n  - name: \"FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      Reproduce FTM on pi0 for visual perturbation types. Compare with LoRA-V and Meta-Brain on visual\n      perturbations. Establish Meta-Brain's value proposition beyond visual-only methods.\n    expected_results:\n      ftm_camera: 63.0\n      ftm_light: 71.0\n      ftm_background: 62.0\n      ftm_noise: 69.0\n      ftm_visual_avg: 66.2\n      lora_v_visual_avg: 67.0\n      meta_brain_visual_avg: 65.5\n      universal_visual_avg: 64.0\n      ftm_on_layout: null\n      ftm_on_robot: null\n      meta_brain_overall_advantage: 1.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      FTM achieves 66.2% on visual perturbations with only ~4K trainable parameters, which is competitive\n      with LoRA-V (67.0%) and actually exceeds Meta-Brain's visual performance (65.5%). FTM's architecture\n      (feature transformation modules) is specifically optimized for visual distribution shifts, giving it\n      an edge on camera/light perturbations. Meta-Brain's soft routing dilutes visual expert weight across\n      all three experts (routing weight 0.52 for LoRA-V on visual inputs), so it underperforms the dedicated\n      LoRA-V. Meta-Brain's only advantage is coverage of spatial/physical types, but with the +1.5% overall\n      gain over universal being non-significant, the coverage argument is weak. In worst tier, FTM's\n      parameter efficiency on visual perturbations (14.5% gain / 4K params) dramatically outperforms\n      Meta-Brain's overall efficiency.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Visual perturbation comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Method      | Params  | Camera | Light | BG   | Noise | Vis Avg |\n          |-------------|---------|--------|-------|------|-------|---------|\n          | Frozen pi0  | 0       | 44.0   | 56.0  | 49.0 | 58.0  | 51.8   |\n          | FTM         | 4K      | 63.0   | 71.0  | 62.0 | 69.0  | 66.2   |\n          | Universal   | 2M      | 57.0   | 68.0  | 61.0 | 70.0  | 64.0   |\n          | LoRA-V      | 2M      | 61.0   | 70.0  | 65.0 | 72.0  | 67.0   |\n          | Meta-Brain  | 6M      | 59.0   | 69.0  | 63.0 | 71.0  | 65.5   |\n      - title: \"Coverage comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Method     | Visual | Spatial | Physical | Overall | Coverage |\n          |------------|--------|---------|----------|---------|----------|\n          | FTM        | 66.2   | —       | —        | —       | Partial  |\n          | Meta-Brain | 65.5   | 64.0    | 72.0     | 66.3    | Full     |\n          | Universal  | 64.0   | 62.0    | 70.8     | 64.8    | Full     |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Visual-perturbation-only bar chart: FTM vs LoRA-V vs Meta-Brain vs Universal. FTM is competitive or better than Meta-Brain on all visual types despite 1500x fewer parameters.\"\n    analysis: >\n      FTM comparison is damaging to Meta-Brain's narrative in worst tier. FTM achieves comparable visual\n      robustness with 4K params (66.2% visual avg) while Meta-Brain requires 6M params for 65.5% on visual\n      types. Meta-Brain's theoretical advantage — full perturbation coverage — yields only +1.5% overall\n      gain over universal. A reviewer could argue: \"just use FTM for visual + universal LoRA for the rest\"\n      as a simpler, more parameter-efficient alternative. In the pivot framing, this comparison supports\n      the design-dimension analysis narrative: different adaptation strategies (feature transformation vs\n      LoRA routing) have different efficiency profiles, and the optimal strategy depends on the perturbation\n      distribution rather than routing quality.\n\n  - name: \"Parameter efficiency analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      Compute gain-over-frozen per method, normalize by trainable parameters. Scatter plot comparison\n      across all methods including cross-paper baselines.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_gain_over_frozen: 12.7\n      meta_brain_params_M: 6.0\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M: 2.12\n      universal_gain_over_frozen: 11.2\n      universal_params_M: 2.0\n      universal_gain_per_M: 5.60\n      ftm_gain_over_frozen_visual: 14.4\n      ftm_params_M: 0.004\n      ftm_gain_per_M_visual: 3600.0\n      vla_gse_gain_over_lora: 12.0\n      vla_gse_params_M: 114.0\n      vla_gse_gain_per_M_over_lora: 0.105\n      meta_brain_gain_over_lora: 1.5\n      meta_brain_gain_per_M_over_lora: 0.25\n    result_rationale: >\n      Parameter efficiency analysis is unfavorable for Meta-Brain in worst tier. Universal LoRA achieves\n      5.60% gain per million parameters — 2.6x more efficient than Meta-Brain (2.12). The 3x parameter\n      budget (6M vs 2M) buys only 1.5% additional gain. FTM dominates on visual-only efficiency\n      (3600 gain/M on visual). When normalized to gain-over-LoRA-baseline (the routing-attributable\n      improvement), Meta-Brain's efficiency drops to 0.25%/M, meaning most of its gain comes from the\n      LoRA components themselves, not the routing mechanism. The scatter plot will show Meta-Brain sitting\n      in an unfavorable region: more parameters than universal LoRA for negligible additional gain, and\n      far from FTM's visual-only efficiency frontier.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Parameter efficiency comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Method         | Backbone   | Params  | Gain/Frozen | Gain/M  | Coverage |\n          |----------------|------------|---------|-------------|---------|----------|\n          | FTM            | pi0        | 4K      | +14.4 (vis) | 3600.0  | Visual   |\n          | Universal LoRA | pi0        | 2M      | +11.2       | 5.60    | Full     |\n          | Meta-Brain     | pi0        | 6M      | +12.7       | 2.12    | Full     |\n          | VLA-GSE*       | Qwen3-VL   | 114M    | +27.6†      | 0.24    | Full     |\n          * different backbone  † estimated gain over Qwen3-VL frozen baseline\n      - title: \"Routing-attributable efficiency\"\n        content: |\n          | Method     | Gain over Uni LoRA | Routing params | Gain/M (routing) |\n          |------------|--------------------|----------------|------------------|\n          | Meta-Brain | +1.5               | 4M (extra)     | 0.375            |\n          | VLA-GSE*   | +12.0              | 114M           | 0.105            |\n          * cross-backbone, not directly comparable\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Log-scale scatter plot: x=trainable params, y=gain-over-frozen. Universal LoRA sits on a better efficiency frontier than Meta-Brain. FTM (visual-only) dominates the low-param region. Meta-Brain is right-shifted from Universal with minimal y-axis gain.\"\n      - \"Marginal efficiency bar chart: gain per additional M params spent beyond universal LoRA. Meta-Brain's marginal efficiency (0.375%/M) is shown as negligible.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The parameter efficiency analysis is the quantitative nail in the routing hypothesis coffin. Universal\n      LoRA achieves 88% of Meta-Brain's total gain (11.2/12.7) with 33% of the parameters (2M/6M). The\n      additional 4M parameters allocated to maintaining separate experts + router yield 0.375% gain per M\n      — a poor return. In the pivot framing, this supports the design-dimension analysis: parameter-matched\n      universal adaptation is a strong default, and routing-based approaches need either higher-capacity\n      experts (rank 32+) or compositional perturbation scenarios to justify the overhead.\n\n  - name: \"Correction Memory ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module. CM adds routing-weight\n      modulation from retrieved exemplars via FAISS.\n    expected_results:\n      meta_brain_no_cm: 66.3\n      meta_brain_with_cm: 66.7\n      cm_ablation_delta: 0.4\n      cm_delta_camera: 0.6\n      cm_delta_light: 0.2\n      cm_delta_background: 0.8\n      cm_delta_noise: 0.0\n      cm_delta_layout: 0.4\n      cm_delta_robot: 0.2\n      cm_pvalue: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      CM adds 0.4% overall — well within noise (p=0.38). Since the base routing weights are already near-uniform\n      (diagonality 0.58), modulating them with retrieved exemplars has minimal effect: modulating a near-flat\n      distribution stays near-flat. CM's theoretical value is sharpening routing confidence for ambiguous cases,\n      but when the experts themselves produce similar outputs, sharper routing does not translate to better\n      correction. The per-type breakdown shows CM's largest effect on background (+0.8%), which is the type with\n      highest cross-expert variance in plan_002, but even this is not significant. CM adds one learned parameter\n      (alpha) and CPU-bound FAISS retrieval — the complexity-to-gain ratio is extremely unfavorable.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"CM ablation per perturbation type\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | No CM  | With CM | Δ    |\n          |------------|--------|---------|------|\n          | Camera     | 59.0   | 59.6    | +0.6 |\n          | Light      | 69.0   | 69.2    | +0.2 |\n          | Background | 63.0   | 63.8    | +0.8 |\n          | Noise      | 71.0   | 71.0    | +0.0 |\n          | Layout     | 64.0   | 64.4    | +0.4 |\n          | Robot      | 72.0   | 72.2    | +0.2 |\n          | **Overall**| **66.3**|**66.7**| **+0.4** |\n          p = 0.38 (paired t-test, not significant)\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Paired bar chart (with/without CM): differences are invisible at chart scale, visually reinforcing the null result.\"\n    analysis: >\n      CM is a clear null result in worst tier. Present as optional module with honest reporting: +0.4%\n      (p=0.38). In the pivot framing, this supports the broader conclusion that the routing bottleneck is\n      expert capacity, not routing precision. Even augmenting routing with episodic memory (retrieved\n      exemplar context) cannot overcome the fundamental limitation that rank-16 experts produce similar outputs.\n      Recommend presenting CM as a \"future work\" direction for higher-capacity expert settings rather than\n      a contribution of this paper.\n\n  - name: \"Router architecture comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare PAR router variants: 2-layer MLP, linear classifier, attention-based router.\n      All on PaliGemma layer 6. Measure routing accuracy and downstream task success.\n    expected_results:\n      mlp_routing_accuracy: 99.3\n      mlp_downstream: 66.3\n      linear_routing_accuracy: 98.1\n      linear_downstream: 66.0\n      attention_routing_accuracy: 99.1\n      attention_downstream: 66.2\n      downstream_range: 0.3\n      routing_accuracy_range: 1.2\n    result_rationale: >\n      All three router architectures produce nearly identical downstream performance (66.0–66.3%, range 0.3%)\n      despite varying routing accuracy (98.1–99.3%). This confirms that routing quality is not the bottleneck:\n      even the simplest linear classifier (98.1% accuracy) achieves 66.0%, only 0.3% below the best MLP router.\n      The attention-based router, despite being more expressive, cannot extract additional routing value because\n      the experts themselves are too similar. The routing accuracy → downstream transfer coefficient is near-zero:\n      a 1.2% improvement in routing accuracy translates to at most 0.3% downstream gain. This dissociation\n      between routing accuracy and downstream success is the strongest evidence that the correction-capacity\n      bottleneck, not routing architecture, limits Meta-Brain's effectiveness.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Router architecture comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Router     | Params (router) | Route Acc (%) | Downstream (%) | Δ from MLP |\n          |------------|-----------------|---------------|-----------------|------------|\n          | Linear     | 6K              | 98.1          | 66.0            | −0.3       |\n          | MLP (2-layer) | 33K          | 99.3          | 66.3            | —          |\n          | Attention  | 132K            | 99.1          | 66.2            | −0.1       |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Dual-axis chart: routing accuracy (left y-axis) vs downstream success (right y-axis) for 3 router types. Routing accuracy varies more than downstream success, showing the disconnect.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Router architecture is irrelevant in worst tier. All variants perform within 0.3% of each other\n      downstream, regardless of routing accuracy differences. This is useful for the design-dimension analysis:\n      it provides evidence that perturbation-diagnostic routing is easy (even a linear classifier suffices) but\n      that the downstream value of routing is limited by expert capacity. Recommend using the linear router in\n      the paper (simplest, nearly identical performance) and framing the comparison as evidence for the\n      \"routing is easy, correction is hard\" narrative.\n\n  - name: \"6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (6 LoRA experts, rank 8 each) vs 3-class coarse routing\n      (3 LoRA experts, rank 16 each) under matched total parameter budget (~6M).\n    expected_results:\n      three_class_overall: 66.3\n      six_class_overall: 65.8\n      six_class_delta: -0.5\n      six_class_camera: 59.4\n      six_class_light: 69.2\n      six_class_background: 62.4\n      six_class_noise: 70.0\n      six_class_layout: 63.6\n      six_class_robot: 70.2\n      six_class_routing_accuracy: 98.8\n      six_class_routing_diagonality: 0.51\n      marginal_gain_per_expert: -0.08\n    result_rationale: >\n      6-class routing performs slightly worse than 3-class (65.8 vs 66.3, Δ=−0.5%). Under a fixed parameter\n      budget, doubling the number of experts halves each expert's rank (rank 8 vs rank 16), reducing individual\n      expert capacity. The finer routing granularity cannot compensate for the reduced per-expert correction\n      power. Additionally, 6-class routing diagonality (0.51) is lower than 3-class (0.58) — with more classes,\n      the routing weights spread more thinly, approaching uniform distribution. The intra-Visual perturbations\n      (camera, light, background, noise) occupy overlapping representation spaces in PaliGemma layer 6\n      (consistent with Phase 0's 6-class accuracy being only marginally above 3-class), so splitting them into\n      separate experts creates artificial boundaries in a continuous representation space. This result validates\n      the 3-class taxonomy and argues against finer granularity at this parameter budget.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"6-class vs 3-class comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Config  | Experts | Rank/Expert | Total Params | Overall | Diag  |\n          |---------|---------|-------------|-------------|---------|-------|\n          | 3-class | 3       | 16          | ~6M         | 66.3    | 0.58  |\n          | 6-class | 6       | 8           | ~6M         | 65.8    | 0.51  |\n          | Δ       |         |             |             | −0.5    | −0.07 |\n      - title: \"6-class per-perturbation results\"\n        content: |\n          | Type       | 3-class | 6-class | Δ    |\n          |------------|---------|---------|------|\n          | Camera     | 59.0    | 59.4    | +0.4 |\n          | Light      | 69.0    | 69.2    | +0.2 |\n          | Background | 63.0    | 62.4    | −0.6 |\n          | Noise      | 71.0    | 70.0    | −1.0 |\n          | Layout     | 64.0    | 63.6    | −0.4 |\n          | Robot      | 72.0    | 70.2    | −1.8 |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Side-by-side routing heatmaps: 3×3 (3-class) vs 6×6 (6-class). 6-class shows more diffuse routing weights with weaker diagonal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Finer granularity hurts under fixed parameter budget. Camera and light benefit marginally (+0.4, +0.2)\n      from dedicated experts, but robot (−1.8) and noise (−1.0) degrade substantially — their rank-8 experts\n      lack capacity for effective correction. This is a capacity-granularity tradeoff: more routing precision ×\n      less correction capacity = net negative. Addresses reviewer concern C5 directly: 3-class is not too coarse;\n      it is actually the better operating point given the rank-16 constraint. Finer granularity would require\n      proportionally higher total rank budget.\n\n  - name: \"Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on SmolVLA to test generalizability of\n      perturbation-diagnostic routing across architectures.\n    expected_results:\n      smolvla_phase0_3class_f1: 0.971\n      smolvla_phase0_best_layer: 4\n      smolvla_phase0_k1_f1: 0.943\n      smolvla_frozen_baseline: 48.2\n      smolvla_universal_lora: 58.6\n      smolvla_meta_brain: 59.8\n      smolvla_gain_over_frozen: 11.6\n      smolvla_gain_over_universal: 1.2\n      smolvla_routing_diagonality: 0.54\n    result_rationale: >\n      Phase 0 replicates strongly on SmolVLA (3-class F1=0.971, K=1 F1=0.943) — perturbation type is\n      diagnosable from frozen activations across architectures. This is the central positive finding for the\n      pivot narrative: VLA internal representations encode perturbation type as a general property, not a\n      pi0 artifact. Phase 1 shows the same pattern as pi0: large universal LoRA gain (+10.4 over frozen),\n      marginal routing gain (+1.2 over universal, not significant). SmolVLA's lower absolute performance\n      (48.2% frozen, 59.8% Meta-Brain) reflects its smaller backbone capacity, but the relative pattern\n      is identical — LoRA adaptation helps, routing doesn't add much. Routing diagonality (0.54) is even\n      weaker than pi0 (0.58), consistent with SmolVLA's slightly lower Phase 0 accuracy. Cross-backbone\n      replication strengthens Phase 0 as the lead contribution and confirms that the routing limitation\n      is a property of the rank-16 LoRA approach, not a backbone-specific artifact.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Cross-backbone Phase 0 comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | Metric            | pi0 (PaliGemma) | SmolVLA      |\n          |-------------------|-----------------|--------------|\n          | 3-class F1        | 0.993           | 0.971        |\n          | Best layer        | 6               | 4            |\n          | K=1 F1            | 1.000           | 0.943        |\n          | Binary AUC        | 1.000           | 0.998        |\n      - title: \"Cross-backbone Phase 1 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Method         | pi0   | SmolVLA | Pattern match? |\n          |----------------|-------|---------|----------------|\n          | Frozen         | 53.6  | 48.2    | —              |\n          | Universal LoRA | 64.8  | 58.6    | —              |\n          | Meta-Brain     | 66.3  | 59.8    | —              |\n          | Δ(MB−Uni)      | +1.5  | +1.2    | ✓ same pattern |\n          | Routing diag   | 0.58  | 0.54    | ✓ same pattern |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"t-SNE comparison: pi0 layer 6 vs SmolVLA layer 4 activations, both showing clear 3-class clustering. Visual evidence that perturbation diagnosability is architecture-general.\"\n      - \"Mirror ablation ladder: pi0 (left) vs SmolVLA (right) with matching relative patterns despite different absolute baselines.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Cross-backbone replication is the strongest evidence for the pivot paper. Phase 0 generalizes (F1>0.97\n      on SmolVLA) while Phase 1's routing limitation also generalizes (Δ=+1.2, not significant). This\n      supports the reframed contribution: \"VLA representations universally encode perturbation type\" (positive\n      finding, lead contribution) + \"diagnostic routing yields marginal downstream gains at rank-16\"\n      (negative-but-informative, generalizes across backbones). SmolVLA's different architecture (different\n      vision encoder, different layer structure) makes the Phase 0 replication a genuine generalization result,\n      not a trivial confirmation. Addresses reviewer concern C3 directly.\n\n  - name: \"LoRA layer placement ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA: early (2-6), mid (6-12), late (12-16), all layers.\n      Test whether type-specific layer placement improves specialization.\n    expected_results:\n      early_layers_overall: 65.8\n      mid_layers_overall: 66.1\n      late_layers_overall: 65.4\n      all_layers_overall: 66.5\n      early_visual_advantage: 0.6\n      mid_spatial_advantage: 0.4\n      late_physical_advantage: 0.2\n      optimal_vs_default_gap: 0.2\n      visual_lora_best_placement: \"early (2-6)\"\n      spatial_lora_best_placement: \"mid (6-12)\"\n      physical_lora_best_placement: \"all\"\n    result_rationale: >\n      Layer placement produces negligible differences (65.4–66.5%, range 1.1%). The hypothesis that Visual LoRA\n      benefits from early layers, Spatial from mid, and Physical from late receives only weak support: Visual\n      expert on early layers gains +0.6% on visual types (compared to default all-layer), Spatial on mid gains\n      +0.4% on layout, but Physical on late layers actually degrades (−0.8% on robot). The \"all layers\" placement\n      (66.5%) is marginally best overall — spreading LoRA across all layers provides the most general correction.\n      At rank 16, the LoRA correction is too low-rank to benefit from layer-specific placement: the correction\n      capacity is distributed thinly regardless of which layers are targeted. The type-specific placement\n      hypothesis would require higher-rank experts where layer-specific corrections could diverge more\n      substantially. This ablation provides mild evidence for the \"experts converge to similar solutions\"\n      hypothesis — even varying their layer targets doesn't produce differentiated behaviors.\n    tables:\n      - title: \"Layer placement overall results\"\n        content: |\n          | Placement    | Layers  | Overall | Visual | Spatial | Physical |\n          |--------------|---------|---------|--------|---------|----------|\n          | Early        | 2-6     | 65.8    | 66.1   | 63.0    | 71.0     |\n          | Mid          | 6-12    | 66.1    | 65.4   | 64.8    | 72.2     |\n          | Late         | 12-16   | 65.4    | 64.8   | 63.2    | 71.8     |\n          | All (default)| 2-16    | 66.5    | 65.5   | 64.0    | 72.0     |\n          | Range        |         | 1.1     | 1.3    | 1.8     | 1.2      |\n      - title: \"Type-specific placement hypothesis test\"\n        content: |\n          | Expert  | Hypothesis        | Best placement | Δ vs all-layers | Confirmed? |\n          |---------|-------------------|----------------|-----------------|------------|\n          | LoRA-V  | Early best        | Early (+0.6)   | +0.6            | Weak       |\n          | LoRA-S  | Mid best          | Mid (+0.8)     | +0.8            | Weak       |\n          | LoRA-P  | Late best         | All (−0.8 late)| −0.8            | No         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Heatmap: (layer placement × perturbation type) success rates. Shows flat landscape with no strong type-layer interaction.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Layer placement is a near-null result. The 1.1% overall range is within seed-level variance, and no\n      type-layer pairing produces a compelling advantage. Physical expert on late layers is actually worse,\n      contradicting the mechanistic hypothesis. At rank 16, the LoRA updates are too constrained to develop\n      layer-specific correction strategies. This supports the overall worst-tier narrative: the rank-16\n      LoRA bottleneck prevents meaningful specialization regardless of routing quality, expert assignment,\n      or layer targeting. Present as supporting evidence for the \"capacity is the bottleneck\" conclusion\n      and a clear direction for future work (higher-rank experts + layer-aware placement).\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b7b30f17-3885-4360-950b-9073de7caf33","total_cost_usd":0.636527,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11382,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":22121,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11382,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":22121,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11382,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11382},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6339,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.006434,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":22121,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11382,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.6300929999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"fdf07444-99f9-4da3-b0ef-b3655c3bce9e"}
